{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfenRavc78zpxn4TqSRyK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate model `heads_default_alternation_ideal`, and it passed the self-check (`OK`). Its smoke-fit ELPD-LOO was −1046.9, about the same as the best model's −1047.1. That fit was 100 draws on one chain, so the two can't really be compared yet. It doesn't show the new term helps.\n\n**Hypothesis:** people don't treat the two faces of the coin the same way. Heads is the expected, \"normal\" outcome, so a sequence with mostly tails looks like a lopsided coin and seems less random. Given two sequences that switch equally often, people pick the one with more heads. This targets the critique's strongest finding (`heads_majority_pref`): in such pairs people chose the heads-heavier sequence 0.567 of the time, against the 0.425 the best model predicts (q = 0.032).\n\n**Model:** I reused the best model, `person_sensitivity_length_scaled_ideal`, and added one shared parameter, `delta`. It weights the difference between the two sequences' share of heads. Everything else is unchanged:\n- each person's own ideal switching rate;\n- each person's own sensitivity to distance from that ideal, scaled by sequence length;\n- a shared penalty for sequences that visibly repeat a short pattern.\n\n**Brief rules:** this is an exploratory slot, and its brief forbids grafting parts of other models together. I count the H/T asymmetry as new information that no existing model uses, not a part taken from another model. But the model is built on the best model's code, and admission may read it as a refinement made in the wrong kind of slot.\n\n**What the check didn't cover:**\n- **Novelty:** if `delta` comes out near zero, the model will predict almost exactly like the best model and be rejected as a near-duplicate.\n- **Convergence:** a single chain can't report R-hat, so I couldn't verify it.\n- **Pareto-k:** 188 of 2,560 trials (7.3%) had Pareto k above 0.5, over the 1% tolerance. So PSIS-LOO was flagged as unreliable on this short fit.\n- **Speed:** the 100-draw fit took 78 seconds, so the full admission fit should finish well inside the 30-minute limit.\n\nThe three files are in `iter_4/candidate_1/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3142,"cache_read_input_tokens":60189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3142,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":718,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"202e714a-fccd-42d6-8e0c-d47d4247bb7a","uuid":"589d7f0f-633d-485e-b107-551bbdf07979","timestamp":"2026-10-03T05:53:13.981Z","request_id":"req_011CfenRaYHKhNY4CG2qbAeN"}
